{"name":"Soul Blue","hex":"#7eadc7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soul","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The spirit or essence of a person usually thought to consist of one's thoughts and personality, often believed to live on after the person's death.","The spirit or essence of anything."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characteristic of or pertaining to African American culture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soul"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soul","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and philosophy, the soul is the immaterial aspect or essence of a living being. It is typically believed to be immortal and to exist apart from the material world. Anthropologists and psychologists have found that most humans believe in the existence of a soul or spirit, and that they have cross-culturally distinguished between the soul and the material body."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Schiavonetti_Soul_leaving_body_1808.jpg/330px-Schiavonetti_Soul_leaving_body_1808.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soul","wikidata":"Q9165"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg/500px-The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg/500px-The_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bhavachakra describing the cycle of saṃsāra; the wheel of life caused by craving to a self, thus giving raise to rebirths in one of the six realms of existence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_wheel_of_life%2C_Buddhism_Bhavachakra.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg/500px-SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg/500px-SoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of a soul being carried to heaven by two angels by William-Adolphe Bouguereau","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoulCarriedtoHeaven.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg/500px-Miraj_by_Sultan_Muhammad.jpg?utm